Pheochromocytoma
Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
Episodic throbbing headaches, Profound diaphoresis, Palpitations/tachycardia, Paroxysmal hypertension

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-034
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, throbbing headache, overwhelming panic, and sudden-onset severe palpitations.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe paroxysmal event characterized by a severe headache, tachycardia, and an extremely high home blood pressure reading, indicative of an acute catecholamine surge. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to evaluate for hypertensive crisis, impending stroke, or acute myocardial infarction, preventing life-threatening end-organ damage before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head is pounding out of my skull, my heart is beating so hard my chest shakes, and I feel like I am going to die right now."
History of Present Illness: Over the past three hours, the patient reports the sudden onset of an episodic severe throbbing headache, accompanied by profound unprovoked diaphoresis. The patient describes sudden onset severe palpitations and resting tachycardia. The spouse notes extreme anxiety, panic attacks, and a sense of impending doom in the patient, alongside marked facial pallor during the acute attack. A home blood pressure cuff recorded a reading of 210/115 mmHg during the peak of the symptoms. The patient reports three similar, albeit less severe, self-resolving episodes over the last two months. They note unintentional significant weight loss of 5 kg recently. Between paroxysms, they frequently experience postural hypotension.
Functional Status: Currently pacing the room, highly agitated, and functionally immobilized by panic and severe headache.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk of hypertensive encephalopathy, acute stroke, or fatal arrhythmia during the paroxysm.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 195/110 mmHg | HR: 125 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 98 percent on room air | Wt: 72 Kg
General: In severe distress, intensely diaphoretic, pale, tremulous, and visibly anxious.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals severe sinus tachycardia with bounding peripheral pulses and severe resting hypertension. No murmurs or gallops. Neurological exam reveals a fine resting tremor of the outstretched hands, brisk reflexes, and no focal motor or sensory deficits. Fundoscopy is deferred due to the patient's severe agitation. Skin is cool and clammy. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Episodic throbbing headaches, Profound diaphoresis, Palpitations/tachycardia, and Paroxysmal hypertension.
Environmental Observations: No obvious external stressors or triggers (e.g., tyramine-containing foods, illicit drug paraphernalia) observed in the immediate environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular prescribed medications. Denies use of over-the-counter decongestants (pseudoephedrine) or illicit stimulants (cocaine/amphetamines).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pheochromocytoma (acute catecholamine crisis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Severe Panic Attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of extreme, life-threatening paroxysmal hypertension (195/110) and profound, soaking diaphoresis, which are objective structural autonomic signs exceeding the physiological norms of a pure psychiatric panic event.

Acute Subarachnoid Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinct paroxysmal nature of the symptoms, the history of similar self-resolving episodes, and the prominent clustering of profound diaphoresis and pallor.

Hyperthyroidism (Thyroid Storm) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The strictly episodic, paroxysmal nature of the attacks rather than a continuous, sustained hypermetabolic state, and the absence of a goitre or exophthalmos.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in the midst of an acute, severe catecholamine crisis highly indicative of a pheochromocytoma, characterized by life-threatening paroxysmal hypertension, tachycardia, and profound autonomic instability. Triage accurately flagged the extremely high blood pressure and severe headache as high-risk for acute end-organ damage. Objective assessment confirmed hypertensive emergency without focal neurological signs. Deferring care until standard business hours would place the patient at imminent, unacceptable risk of a fatal cerebrovascular or cardiovascular event. Immediate out-of-hours evaluation was clinically mandated to assess acute stability, avoid contraindicated therapies (like isolated beta-blockers), and arrange immediate emergency transfer, fulfilling urgent MBS criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Activated 000 for immediate priority emergency ambulance response due to hypertensive emergency and risk of end-organ damage. Strictly avoided the administration of beta-blockers, which can precipitate a fatal unopposed alpha-adrenergic hypertensive crisis in this setting. Kept the patient strictly rested in a seated, upright position in a quiet, dark room to minimize sensory stimulation. Prepared clinical handover for paramedics and the receiving emergency physician detailing the high suspicion for pheochromocytoma requiring emergent alpha-blockade (e.g., phentolamine or phenoxybenzamine).
Positive Behavioral Change: By sitting quietly in a darkened room without talking (action), you minimize external sensory input to your nervous system (cause), which directly helps prevent further spikes in your already dangerously high blood pressure while awaiting the ambulance (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nil. Requires immediate acute medical admission.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the paramedics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance is en route. If the patient develops crushing chest pain, sudden weakness on one side of their body, slurred speech, or loss of consciousness, inform the 000 dispatcher immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The extreme, episodic nature of your blood pressure and heart rate strongly suggests a rare tumor on your adrenal gland pumping out massive amounts of adrenaline. This is a medical emergency requiring highly specialized hospital care.
Clinical Course: These attacks will continue to occur and pose a severe risk of stroke or heart attack until the tumor is definitively located and surgically removed, following careful medical preparation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to stand up or walk, as your blood pressure may suddenly drop when the attack ends. Do not take any of your usual medications or any over-the-counter pain relief. Wait quietly for the paramedics.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A sudden, massive surge of adrenaline from an internal source has caused your blood vessels to clamp down and your heart to race. The hospital will use highly specific intravenous medications to safely block these adrenaline receptors and bring your blood pressure down without causing further harm.
Actionable Steps: Rest quietly in the chair. Do not move around. The spouse should gather any previous medical records noting similar episodes for the hospital team.

